Hashes built-in object shapes so the register property cache can serve them (W2c)

The per-function register property cache refuses a receiver whose shape is
unhashed, because an unhashed shape is mutated in place whatever its reference
count, so a cached reference to one could watch its slot move. The built-in
objects are exactly the unhashed ones, which is why their reads fell out of the
cache. Hashing the shape keeps the cache's ownership rule instead of bypassing
it, and makes the shape stable.

  * quickjs.c: regvm_shape_hashify hashes a shape on first use -- growing the
    runtime shape hash table when needed and replaying the same fold
    js_new_shape2 uses, so find_hashed_shape_prop still verifies every property
    atom and flag; regvm_pic_fill calls it for the receiver shape and, one hop
    up, for the holder shape.

This patch does not change the register format or the bytecode version.
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -22207,6 +22207,37 @@
     e->kind = kind;
 }
 
+/* Hash an unhashed shape in place so the per-function PIC can hold it.
+ *
+ * The PIC stores COUNTED references, which pin a shape against being freed but
+ * NOT against being mutated in place: `js_shape_prepare_update` returns
+ * immediately for an unhashed shape whatever its refcount, so a cached slot can
+ * watch a property's flags change or its offset move. A hashed shape is
+ * copy-on-write instead -- once the PIC holds a reference the refcount is >= 2,
+ * so any update clones rather than mutating in place -- which is exactly the
+ * invariant the hit path needs. Reading a depth-0/1 site therefore hashes the
+ * receiver's shape. The hash is recomputed from the property list because
+ * `sh->hash` is only maintained while `is_hashed` (add_shape_property updates
+ * it only in that branch).
+ */
+static void regvm_shape_hashify(JSRuntime *rt, JSShape *sh)
+{
+    uint32_t h, i;
+
+    if (sh->is_hashed)
+        return;
+    if (2 * (rt->shape_hash_count + 1) > rt->shape_hash_size)
+        resize_shape_hash(rt, rt->shape_hash_bits + 1);
+    h = shape_initial_hash(sh->proto);
+    for (i = 0; i < sh->prop_count; i++) {
+        JSShapeProperty *pr = get_shape_prop(sh) + i;
+        h = shape_hash(shape_hash(h, pr->atom), pr->flags);
+    }
+    sh->hash = h;
+    sh->is_hashed = true;
+    js_shape_hash_link(rt, sh);
+}
+
 static void regvm_pic_fill(JSContext *ctx, JSFunctionBytecode *b,
                            JSRegPIC **ppic, JSValue recv, JSAtom atom, int site)
 {
@@ -22277,6 +22308,15 @@
     rsh = p->shape;
     slot = (uint32_t)(pr - holder->prop);
 
+    /* A depth-0/1 site hashes its receiver shape so the per-function PIC can
+       cache it (see regvm_shape_hashify). Depth-2 sites stay with the global
+       stub cache, which does not need a hashed shape. */
+    if (depth <= 1) {
+        regvm_shape_hashify(rt, rsh);
+        if (hsh)
+            regvm_shape_hashify(rt, hsh);
+    }
+
     /* The site's state before the install below; the install is what can newly
        set the dead state, so sample it first. A site with no table yet (pic
        NULL) is not dead. */
